# Line endings for the files whose interpreter cares. Everything else keeps Git's default
# handling (each machine's core.autocrlf), as before. Committed blobs are always LF; these rules
# only decide what a checkout writes. docs/MOVING_DATA.md and docs/HOSTING.md.
#
# Linux runs these: always LF, even in a checkout made on Windows (a CR at a line end breaks
# bash, sh and systemd, e.g. "bash\r: No such file or directory").
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf

# Windows runs these: always CRLF (cmd.exe can misread labels and goto targets in a file with LF
# line ends; Windows Script Host and Windows PowerShell scripts get the same treatment).
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.vbs     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
